= Classification =

== Overview ==
'''Classification''' is the process or system of assigning items, observations, entities or concepts to categories/classes according to stated criteria, rules, models or learned patterns.

Classification appears in:
* biology;
* libraries;
* medicine;
* statistics;
* machine learning;
* law;
* information systems;
* everyday reasoning.

Different kinds of classification solve different problems.

== Classification and categories ==
Classification requires some target [[Category|categories]].

The categories may be:
* predefined;
* discovered during analysis;
* hierarchical;
* overlapping;
* mutually exclusive;
* fuzzy.

The quality of classification depends partly on whether the categories are meaningful for the intended use.

== Classification scheme ==
A '''classification scheme''' is an organized system for placing items into categories.

Examples include:
* library classification;
* disease classification;
* product categories;
* document subject classes.

A classification scheme can include notation, hierarchy, rules and indexing conventions.

== Classification and taxonomy ==
A [[Taxonomy]] organizes categories or concepts.

Classification is the act/process of assigning things using such a structure.

Therefore:

<pre>
taxonomy = organization of categories
classification = assignment using categories
</pre>

In everyday usage the words are sometimes blurred.

== Statistical classification ==
In statistics and machine learning, classification predicts a discrete label from data.

A classifier can be learned from labeled examples.

Examples:
* spam / not spam;
* disease category;
* image class.

This is one specialized form of classification.

A statistical classifier does not automatically define an ontology of the world.

== Rule-based classification ==
Classification can also use explicit rules.

For example:

<pre>
if age < 18 → minor
</pre>

Rule-based systems make criteria inspectable, but edge cases and changing definitions can require revision.

== Ontological classification ==
Description-logic reasoners use "classification" in a more specialized sense: computing the subclass hierarchy implied by ontology axioms.

This does not mean assigning statistical labels to observations.

The same word therefore names different processes in different fields.

== Supervised and unsupervised grouping ==
Supervised classification learns to predict predefined labels.

Clustering groups items by similarity without predefined labels.

Clustering is therefore not classification in the strict supervised-learning sense, though clusters may later become categories.

== Multi-label classification ==
An item can belong to several categories.

For example, a research paper can be both:
* computer science;
* biology;
* statistics.

Multi-label classification represents this explicitly.

This differs from forcing exactly one class.

== Classification errors ==
Errors include:

* false positives;
* false negatives;
* category ambiguity;
* inconsistent criteria;
* label leakage;
* distribution shift;
* biased category definitions.

A technically accurate classifier can still be socially or scientifically poor if the categories themselves are invalid or inappropriate.

== Classification and ontology ==
An [[Ontology]] can supply formal classes and relationships used for classification.

But a classifier can exist without an ontology, and an ontology can do far more than classify.

The two concepts should not be collapsed.

== Epistemic notes ==
Classification creates or applies boundaries. Those boundaries can reflect nature, convention, institutional goals or modeling convenience. Their status should be made explicit when consequential.

